cs-04.theory:
# Making the program decide

In the previous lesson the program always did the same calculations. Now it will choose **different paths** depending on the data.

## Comparisons and bool

Comparisons produce a `bool` (`true` or `false`):

```csharp
nota >= 7     // greater than or equal
nota < 5      // less than
x == 0        // equal (two = signs)
x != 0        // different
```

Careful: `=` **assigns** a value; `==` **compares**. In C#, `if (x = 0)` doesn't even compile, which avoids a classic error from other languages.

## if, else if, else

```csharp
if (temperatura > 30)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Calor");
}
else if (temperatura > 15)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Agradável");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Frio");
}
```

The conditions are tested **in order**, from top to bottom. The first true one runs its block and the others are **ignored**. That is why the order matters: in `else if (temperatura > 15)` we already know the temperature is **not** greater than 30, so we don't need to repeat that.

The braces `{ }` delimit the block. With a single statement they are optional, but always using them avoids bugs when you later add a second line.

## Combining conditions

- `&&` (AND): true only if **both** are true.
- `||` (OR): true if **at least one** is true.
- `!` (NOT): inverts.

```csharp
if (idade >= 18 && temCarteira)
    Console.WriteLine("Pode dirigir");

if (nota < 0 || nota > 10)
    Console.WriteLine("Nota inválida");
```

C# uses **short-circuit evaluation**: in `a && b`, if `a` is false, `b` isn't even evaluated. This lets you safely write `if (b != 0 && a / b > 2)`.

## Validate first

A good habit is to handle the invalid cases **first**. That way the rest of the code can assume the data is correct.

## Common mistakes

- Writing `if (nota >= 7);` with a semicolon: the `;` ends the `if` and the block always runs.
- Wrong order: testing `nota >= 5` before `nota >= 7` makes a grade of 9 fall into "Recuperação".
- Comparing `double` with `==` after calculations: values like `0.1 + 0.2` are not exactly `0.3`. Prefer `>=` and `<`.

cs-04.task:
Read a grade (a decimal number, with a dot, e.g. `6.5`) and show **one** line:

- `Nota inválida` if the grade is less than 0 or greater than 10;
- `Aprovado` if the grade is greater than or equal to 7;
- `Recuperação` if it is greater than or equal to 5 (and less than 7);
- `Reprovado` otherwise.

Example: input `6.5` produces `Recuperação`.

cs-05.theory:
# Repeating statements

With `if` the program chooses paths. With **loops** it repeats a block many times, without copying code.

## while

`while` repeats **as long as** the condition is true:

```csharp
int contador = 3;
while (contador > 0)
{
    Console.WriteLine(contador);
    contador--;   // same as contador = contador - 1
}
// 3, 2, 1
```

If nothing inside the loop changes the condition, it never ends (an **infinite loop**). Always ask yourself: what makes this loop stop?

## for

When you know **how many times** to repeat, `for` puts the three parts in one place:

```csharp
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
{
    Console.WriteLine(i);   // 0 1 2 3 4
}
```

1. `int i = 0`: runs once, at the start.
2. `i < 5`: tested before each iteration; if false, the loop ends.
3. `i++`: runs at the end of each iteration.

Counting from `0` to `n - 1` is the standard in C#, because arrays (lesson 7) start at index 0.

## Accumulators

The most common pattern with loops is to **accumulate** a result:

```csharp
int n = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());
int soma = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
{
    int x = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());
    soma += x;   // soma = soma + x
}
```

Notice: the variable `soma` is declared **outside** the loop. If it were inside, it would be recreated (reset to zero) on every iteration and wouldn't be visible after the loop. This is the concept of **scope**: a variable only exists inside the braces where it was declared.

## Largest and smallest

To find the largest value, start with the **first** element (not with 0, which would fail if all were negative). Another safe option is to start with `int.MinValue`, the smallest possible `int`:

```csharp
int maior = int.MinValue;
// inside the loop:
if (x > maior) maior = x;
```

## break and continue

- `break` leaves the loop immediately.
- `continue` skips to the next iteration.

## Common mistakes

- `i <= n` instead of `i < n`: one iteration too many.
- Initializing the largest with `0`: wrong for lists of only negatives.
- Forgetting the case of 0 elements: the loop doesn't run and "maior" keeps a meaningless value. Handle that case first.

cs-08.theory:
# Modeling data with classes

Until now each piece of data was a loose variable. But in the real world data goes together: a student has a **name** and **grades**. A **class** puts data (state) and behavior (methods) together in a single type, created by you.

## Declaring a class

```csharp
class Aluno
{
    public string Nome { get; }          // read-only property
    private List<int> notas = new List<int>(); // private field

    public Aluno(string nome)            // constructor
    {
        Nome = nome;
    }

    public void AdicionarNota(int nota)
    {
        notas.Add(nota);
    }

    public double Media()
    {
        int soma = 0;
        foreach (int n in notas) soma += n;
        return (double)soma / notas.Count;
    }
}
```

- A **field** (`notas`) stores data inside the object.
- A **property** (`Nome { get; }`) looks like a field from the outside, but controls access: here it can only be read, and it is set in the constructor.
- The **constructor** has the name of the class and runs when the object is created with `new`.
- `public` can be used from outside; `private` only inside the class. Hiding the details (**encapsulation**) lets you change the internal workings without breaking whoever uses the class.

```csharp
Aluno a = new Aluno("Ana");
a.AdicionarNota(8);
a.AdicionarNota(7);
Console.WriteLine(a.Media()); // 7.5
```

Now the methods are **not** `static`: they work with the data of **one specific object**. Each `new Aluno(...)` has its own grades.

## List<T>

`List<T>` is an array that grows: `Add`, `Count`, index access `lista[i]`. The `T` is the type of the elements (`List<int>`, `List<Aluno>`).

## Dictionary<TKey, TValue>

A dictionary associates **keys** with **values** and finds a key in practically constant time, without going through everything:

```csharp
var porNome = new Dictionary<string, Aluno>();
if (!porNome.TryGetValue("Ana", out Aluno aluno))
{
    aluno = new Aluno("Ana");
    porNome["Ana"] = aluno;
}
aluno.AdicionarNota(9);
```

`TryGetValue` returns `true` and fills `aluno` if the key exists. Accessing `porNome["Bia"]` with a nonexistent key throws `KeyNotFoundException`.

**Careful with the order:** the `Dictionary` documentation **does not guarantee** the order in which keys are traversed. When order matters, also store the objects in a `List` in the order they appeared, and use the dictionary only to **find** them quickly.

## References

Classes are **reference types**: the variable holds an "address" of the object. The same `Aluno` can be in the list and in the dictionary at the same time; adding a grade through one path shows up through the other, because it is the **same** object.

## Common mistakes

- Dividing `int` by `int` in the average (lesson 3): use `(double)soma`.
- Forgetting `new` and using a `null` variable: `NullReferenceException`.
- Relying on the dictionary's order.

cs-10.theory:
# Algorithms and the cost of running them

In the previous lessons we learned to **write** programs. Now we will learn to **compare** programs: two correct algorithms can have wildly different costs when the input grows.

## Big-O notation

Measuring in seconds depends on the machine. Instead, we count **basic operations** as a function of the input size `n`, and look only at the term that **dominates** when `n` is large, ignoring constants. Formally, `f(n) = O(g(n))` if there are constants `c` and `n0` such that `f(n) <= c·g(n)` for all `n >= n0`.

- `O(1)`: constant. Accessing `arr[i]`.
- `O(log n)`: logarithmic. Each step **halves** the problem.
- `O(n)`: linear. Walking through the array once.
- `O(n log n)`: the good sorts (merge sort, and `Array.Sort` in practice).
- `O(n²)`: quadratic. Two nested loops over the data.
- `O(2^n)`: exponential, like the Tower of Hanoi from lesson 9.

For `n = 1 000 000`: `log n` is about 20, `n log n` about 20 million, and `n²` is a trillion. The choice of algorithm matters more than the speed of the computer.

## Insertion sort

The idea is that of someone arranging cards in their hand: we keep the beginning of the array **sorted** and insert each new element in the right place, shifting the larger ones one position to the right.

```csharp
static void InsertionSort(int[] v)
{
    for (int i = 1; i < v.Length; i++)
    {
        int atual = v[i];
        int j = i - 1;
        while (j >= 0 && v[j] > atual)
        {
            v[j + 1] = v[j];
            j--;
        }
        v[j + 1] = atual;
    }
}
```

**Invariant**: at the start of each iteration, `v[0..i-1]` is sorted. It holds at the beginning (a single element is sorted) and each iteration preserves it; at the end, `i == v.Length` and the whole array is sorted. This is how you **prove** an algorithm is correct.

**Cost**: in the worst case (array in descending order) the inner loop runs `1 + 2 + ... + (n-1) = n(n-1)/2` times: **O(n²)**. In the best case (already sorted) it is **O(n)**. It is **stable** (equal elements keep their order) and sorts **in place**, with O(1) extra memory. Note the strict `>`: with `>=` it would no longer be stable.

Arrays in C# are reference types: the method changes the very array it received.

## Binary search

In a **sorted** array, we compare the target with the middle element and discard half at each step:

```csharp
int ini = 0, fim = v.Length - 1, achou = -1;
while (ini <= fim)
{
    int meio = ini + (fim - ini) / 2;
    if (v[meio] < alvo) ini = meio + 1;
    else
    {
        if (v[meio] == alvo) achou = meio;
        fim = meio - 1;   // keep searching to the left
    }
}
```

This variant finds the **first** occurrence of the target, even with duplicates. After `k` steps about `n / 2^k` candidates remain, so it takes at most about `log2(n) + 1` steps: **O(log n)**. A million elements: around 20 steps, versus up to a million for linear search.

Why `ini + (fim - ini) / 2` and not `(ini + fim) / 2`? Because the sum can **overflow** the `int` in huge arrays; a real bug that lived for years in famous libraries.

## Is it worth sorting in order to search?

Sorting costs O(n log n) (with a good algorithm) and each search afterwards costs O(log n). For **one** search, the linear O(n) one is cheaper. For **many** searches, sorting once pays off. Thinking this way is the heart of algorithm analysis.

## Common mistakes

- Binary search on an unsorted array: meaningless results.
- `while (ini < fim)` instead of `<=`: misses the single-element case.
- Forgetting `j >= 0` in insertion sort: `IndexOutOfRangeException`.
